# Commit message convention

This project follows [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)
with project-specific scope conventions inferred from the existing git log.

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

- **Lowercase** for type, scope, and subject.
- **No trailing period** on the subject line.
- **Imperative mood** for the subject (`add`, `fix`, `replace`, `make`).
- Keep the subject line under ~72 characters.
- Separate header, body, and footer with blank lines.

## Allowed types

| Type       | Use for                                           |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `feat`     | New user-facing feature or capability             |
| `fix`      | Bug fix                                           |
| `refactor` | Internal restructure with no behavior change      |
| `style`    | Formatting / visual-only tweaks (no logic change) |
| `chore`    | Tooling, deps, configs, lockfiles                 |
| `docs`     | Documentation only                                |
| `test`     | Tests only                                        |
| `perf`     | Performance improvement                           |

## Scope conventions

Scope reflects **where** the change lives. Patterns observed in this repo:

- **App name**: `web`, `mobile`
- **Package name**: `core`, `i18n`, `design-system`
- **Sub-area with slash**: `web/home`, `web/routes`, `web/puzzle-types`
- **Domain feature**: `puzzles`, `puzzle-types`, `puzzle-illustration`
- **Tooling**: `deps`, `eslint`, `next.config`

Pick the most specific scope that still reads clearly. If the change spans
many areas, drop the scope (`feat: ...`).

## Body usage

- **Skip the body** for small, self-evident changes (this is the default in
  this repo's history).
- **Add a body** when the change is broad, touches multiple areas, or the
  "why" isn't obvious from the subject. Use a bullet list to enumerate
  notable changes:

  ```
  refactor(web): reorganize home page sections

  - extract hero, features, and showcase into separate section components
  - move shared motion variants to shared/lib/motion.ts
  - replace inline framer-motion configs with the new variants
  ```

- Bullets should be short, imperative, and lowercase-leading.
- Use the body to explain rationale or trade-offs, not to restate the diff.

## Footer (optional)

- `BREAKING CHANGE: <description>` for breaking API/behavior changes.
- `Refs #123` / `Closes #123` to link issues.

## Good examples (from this repo)

- `feat(puzzle-types): show coming-soon as corner ribbon to keep illustrations visible`
- `refactor(web/routes): split puzzle paths into /browse and /play`
- `feat(puzzles): give the four sample puzzles 3x3..6x6 board sizes`
- `fix(eslint): update tsconfigRootDir to use dirname and fileURLToPath for better path resolution`
- `chore(deps): add pnpm lockfile`

## Bad examples

- `Update files` — no type, vague subject.
- `feat: Added new feature.` — capitalized, past tense, trailing period.
- `fix(web/home/sections/hero): typo` — overly nested scope; use `web/home`.
- `refactor: lots of changes` — broad change without a body explaining what.
